er of Harin, is an ancient “ Base Leinster” name. 2 Honey was a staple commodity at Itm in formoyll for eVy kowe iiijd. ey croo of shep i. shep. ey croo of swyn a hogg, graunted by Brene M°Tirrelagh M*Donghe yeree vnto Kildare. Itm upon Clanmalaghlin Roo for the defence of Ballydawoke, the Parke, Garran ne boly, Croyll, and the half of Ballygnevyn, and Kyl- bracan, Kyllermoy iiijd of eVy cow, a mutton of eVy cro of shepe, and a swyne of eVy croo of swyne at e\y halontide. Of the fferaneheglyshe of eVy cow iiijd. Vpon the vicar of Chircheton iiijd of eVy cow, a swyne of eVy croo of swyne, and a mutton of e\’'y croo of shepe belonging vnto him. Of eVy cow in the Cargyn iiijd of eVy crow, ® mutton of eVvy crow of shepe, and a swyne ot eVy croo of swyne. Itm of the Loran in likewyse. Itin of Ballyvaghlin in like mait. Itm of Graige Arde in lyke mat to be payed all at eVy halontide. Itin on the sonnys of Donnogh Makdon- nok OShowylle in the Burgache and in Syre- bege one eVv cowe iiijd. Itm on eVy crowe of shepe a shepe. $ QO’Doran was hereditary brehon. 4 A leech, surgeon, or physician. O’Fannin(?) was hereditary mediciner to the Leys tribe, for which he held that time, when sugar was unknown, when metheglin was appreciated, and when honey was probably mingled with the still favourite mountain drink, viz., milk and whiskey. that “‘moncy,” i.e., moor, in fee simple. 5 «* Ossory.”—i. e. Osraighce., the ter- ritory of Mac Giollaphatric, then con- terminous with the modern barony of Upper Ossory. 125 Iti vpon eVye crowe of swyne a hogge to be payde at hallontyde graunted for terme of lyff beyng p’sent Jamys Boyf, Jamys Kyng, Edmone Sex, Wat Long, graunted by Wone and Tyrrelaghe M‘donnogh in the name of all ther brethern the xix. day of Novébre the xx1j. | yere of King H. viii'™. Fotto xvi3. Oregane,! O dones Countre. Itin vpon Twosmeré yerly vi. Rudderf to Willam Roo O Dympsy, } Kildare. B. It vppon the Vikere of the same yerly 1). Ruddres. An indent’ from Cayre ODoyn vppon