v pon evye plowe lande in Cloncolgyn yerely a Rodder. In feran O Morgan jj. Rodd?s yerly. Itih vpon eVy plowe lande of Clonecolghyn yerly a Reudder. | Folio xtx. Ferkeall,’ O’ Sfulmoyes Countre. Itim in Moyanna vpon eVy plowland yerly at Mich for xlix. ploland( at xiid. the ploland yevin by Brene M‘Shane O Mulmoy vnto the said Erll. Sm* xxixeé, Itm vpon Rory O Doygyn yerely at hallomas 11j. Rudders. Itm vpon his sonnys yerely vis. iijd. Kynalfyagh,’ Mageoghegans Countre. lii** % xii. plow lands in Kynnalfiagh, by the declaration of Nycholas Walshe, a° 1560. [ This is in a later hand ]. Paden OHurgy, ceiver. [.....] by the hands of Paden OHurge. 1 “Offaly."—A full account of this ter- ritory, Ui-Failyhe, will accompany the above-mentioned map. 2 This and the third item below it are crossed out in the original. 3 Ferkeall.”—This denomination is explained in a learned note to the Irish Topographical Poems to mean “ Virt cel- larum seu potius ecclesiarum ;” to have been preserved in ‘‘ Fircal,” a barony in the King’s County, now known as ‘‘Eelish,” from the Irish eaglap, a church; and to have comprised not only this barony, but also the baronies of Bal- lycowan and Ballyboy. It belonged to Re Itm, upon every ploland yerly in the said : { countre at Mich xiid. yevin by Leynagh* M‘keoghane unto the said Erll. Itm, in Ardnurghir,® a plowland, which was yevin to Cahir OConor, Capitayne of his the O’Maolmhuaidhs, anglicé O’Molloys. 4° Kynalfiyagh.”"—Cinel Fhiachach, an- glicised Kenaliagh or Kinalea. This coun- try is accurately defined on an old map ene in the second volume of the rish ‘“‘State Papers,” showing it to have been twelve miles long and seven broad. 6 Laighnech, anglicised Lynagh, i. e., ‘*the Lagenian,” or Leinsterman. He became the Mageoghegan in 1493. 6 Ardnurcher, Ath-an-urchair, or the Horse-Leap. The remains of the castle here show it to have been a large and stately edifice. See Professor O’Curry’s “ Lectures,” Appendix, pp. 993, G40. 127 -..... by the hands of Paden OHurge. dare; and after that, Leynagh Makeogane, capitayne of the said countre, with all his sept of Sleought Hew Bowy' did reles their hole 1 Aedh, or Hugh, Buidhe, i. e., “the ro, grandfather of Leynagh. This ugh “the yellow” MacGeoghan was lord of Kinel Fiachach, pronounced “ Ki- Nnelea ;” as was his son Conall, who was slain in 1470. Laighneach assumed the chieftaincy on the death of his brother, anno 1463.—(“ Four Masters.”) There is a curious Irish deed, written on a small square piece of parchment, insert- ed between pages 37 and 38 of the original Rental. It is as follows, froma copy made by the late Professor Eugene O’Curry, with a translation made by that eminent Irish scholar for the Society :— GQmen. In Oe nomine. dp 6 po con and bEuain Sens6io mac Tomdaip, mic Seon .1. lanla Chil- le bana gu vcurP (?) sperm na Rata Onipe6gu1sf 6 Uaignech, mac Conta, mic eda Ourte .1. cisenna Cinedil pocehees ocur coil plecca Ceda uve, ocup plecca Néill, ocup Phe- ansal Ruaw Gus pin .t. m clanla vo cosgbail Coille Tobuin pia upldim Féin 6 pil SConcubain do pannowms Fednann cloweim vo $éanam 1, ocup aq scun vocum céna do sénam ne Mag Cochagdn, ocur acd 6 Mas Gochagdn von tanla, a cenn 1§ tus 06, DO Ginn In Comtpnulm Pim vo sc- nam .. vr. mang an Choilt Tobuin Fém vo Hndt, vo Féin ocup da Mac ind Seaguld, ocup va Plucc; ocuPp va mbead cogad an m lanla ne nicfad alep mia, Coll Tobuin oagsail 06 do- cum in 60516; ocup da mbead nann aig! DO nicpad alep pia, comiunli in Tanlad vo sénamh an Colt Tobuin do BeIG an maiti PP mM pann; ocur va mbead naé cicpad do Mhag Eocagsan no va pPlicéc Coll Tobmp do bdann- vacr, in clanla vo Gun banpva mnt. “This is the way in which Garrct, son of Thomas, son of John, the Earl of Kildare, obtained at first hold of Rath Driscoyhighi from Laighnech son of Conall [MacGeoghan] son of Aedh Buidhe, the Lord of Cenel Fiachaidh; and the descendants of Acdh Buidhe, and the descendants of Niall, and of Fergal Ruaidh, were consenting parties to it. Viz.: The Earl is to take Coill Tobuir into his own hands from the Sil Chon- chabair [race of O Conor] who coveted to make sword land of it, and is to force themto make terms with the Macgeoghe- gan. And there is from MacGeoghegan to the Earl, in addition to the [above mentioned] land, half a mark out of Coill Tobuir itself in perpetuity to him- self and to his son after him, and to his posterity. Andif there should be war against the Earl, andthatitshould bead- vantageous to him to get Coill Tobuir for the purposes of the war, or if he should have an ally to whom it would be advan- tageous, the Ear] is to decide whether Coill Tobuir is to be given to such ally. And if it should happen that MacGeo- ghan or his descendants should not be able to defend Coill Tobuir, the Earl [is bound] to put defenders into it.” This deed, which is written on a piece of vellum not larger than the palm of a man’s hand, is so curious a specimen of Gaelic conveyancing that a tacsimile is laid before the reader. See plate faciag page III supra, facsimile No. 2, The en- dorsement is as follows :— ‘“‘Lands and rents yevin by Laynaghe Mageoghane, the cheff_ of his nacon, lord of Kinaliaghe to G'frod fitz Thom:s Erle of Kildare. vis, vill’. by the yere on Kyltobbyr and the castell of the same to be at the Erelis comavndement.” Radrissoge, one of the denominations mentioned in this curious deed, as ap- pears by an inquisition of the reign of James I. (Co. Westmeath), contained sixty acres, and belonged to Hugh Mac Geoghan. Coill Tobbuir is correctly transluted ‘the Wellwood" in the same document. Fergal Roe MacGeoghecan died in 1458 according to Mac Firbis’s Annals. A succeeding Mageoghegan, namely, Conall mac Conchobhair, made on the 20th August, 1520, a similar curious compact with “The Fox” (Stonnach), Gleanefedok. Corinnahir. Ballynghomyn. 128 title therto to Gerald fitz Gerald, son to the said Gerald. Itm, the said Gerald yave to Ovyn begg ODala, in full acquitting of the pledge that he had upon half the Genyol kille ix. marcf money, and to Thole OHuggin in like manner for the ful payment of the other half vi. acres of tethe in full payment of vi. melshe kyne. It, Malaghlyn mac Hew M‘Keoghane yave the said Gerald oon cartron in Gleanfe- dok for vi. marcf money. Itm, another car- ton in Gleanefedok in pledg of xii. in calf kyne, which Ferall dofe OFuore had payd him. Itm, vpon a plowland calde Corinnahir in pledge xviii. kyne, which was payd to Kahyre m°Art OKonor. Itm, a plowland in Balyncomyn, in pledg Ix. kyne, by the jugment of Shane M°Egan,' for a pray that the Cosny O Moylowe is sonys nade on Moryartagh M°Geoghegan is sonys, for that Conghowr M‘Brenrowe was gylte of ye said pray; and the said Moryartazh sonys gave the same to Gerald fitz Gerald Erle of Kildare, in amends of the dethe of Moryartagh m° Hue Mageoghegan, whych was sleyne in treyson by Nele M‘Moryarte. Itin, half a plowland in Down Owyyr, in M°Geoghegan is sonys, for the kylling of the Nychas Walsh, Recevyr 1 hres a has Ms at oa la ) ° said Moriartagh. chief of Munterhagan, by which the former chieftain was to be deemed lord over the Fox, and his country. One of the covenants is to the effect that whenever a war, or disturbance, came either upon Mageoghegan or upon the Fox, on account of which one of them might bring his forces with him out of territory, the other should bear the pro- portion of his territory of the expense of the bonayht men, or waged soldiery. The new chief was to do his utmost to protect and shelter the Fox, and every person in his country, both small and great. Whenever any part of their territory should be unjustly, or with de- fault of rent, detained by tenants, with- out paying any or the usual rent, Ma- georhegan was to do his utmost endea- vour to recover such part of the terri- tory. Another covenant implies that the Earl of Kildare had customarily exerted his power in assisting such chiefs as were under his banner in re- covering dues from their interior septs or slioghts. ‘* Whenever,” the clause runs, “the Earl of Kildare declines to reco- ver it ie rent], Mageoghegan is bound to lend his utmost endeavours to reco- ver it.” (See ‘* Miscellany” of the Irish Archeological Society, vol. i., p. 179). This remarkable covenant shows that Lord Kildare stood virtually in the place of the Crown, as the power possessing strength sufficient to enforce law. He was Ard-Righ, or supreme king, of the south-eastern and midland chieftains, and they were his Uir-righe, or sub-kings, in almost as complete a political rela- tionship as that in which O'Neill and bis vassals, O'Cathain, Mac Mahon, Ma- guire, O'Hanlon, &c., stood. ' The Mae Eyans weze a numerous tribe of Brehons, or Gaelic Judges. 129 Item, the half plowland of the Kyllye, por- Kyllye. _ chasyd of Bryen OBrenan, for [.. .], whereof Padyn OHurge payd him xiii. in calf kyne. To Brene OKing, at Portlest, the xvi. day of May, anno xvii.,H. viii", iiiils. iijs. vid. which was the last som* of xxx. kyne that he and his brethrin had vpo Ballanorghyr in Glannevedog. A cartron in pledg of xii. kyne which Padyn OHurke paid to Feraile Duff of Nyr, and an other cartron which Moy- lachlyn M‘hew did yeve unto the said Gerald and his ayrs for ever, wytnis Padyn OHurke and others.' Itm, upon Aghyncnoyke in Moyraghe xls. which were payd to Kayr O’Molmoy. Knocnocorr. Itm, the half plowland of Knocnocor and the cartron of Feragh fyne, in pledg of jij. score and ten kyne from One M‘Morirte and Moyllaghlyn M*Rore M‘Gyoghegan by the jugement of Conle and Congho’ Ballagh Nic: Walshe, Reiceivyor. } M*Kegan for breking of Geralde Erle of Kyl- dare is slantyaght? vpon Feral M°Owin. Wyt- tenys, Rosse M°Geohigan, abbot of Kilbyan, Pte M*Kygan, Padyn OHurgy, and Thomas | Albenorgh. Itm, paid the sonys of Edmond ODowlyng by thands of William Row ODaly and Tole OKyng, xx/t. Irish mony, in the quiting of a plowlandecalled GnywLeackyvoriagan by Kyl- dromkryachragh in Kynnalfiagh in the countie o